 hook='One cup in the morning, one after lunch, one more at 4 PM... ☕ What if you drank coffee every single day? Is it hurting you or helping you? KOMO checks the science.',
 how='Caffeine works by blocking adenosine, a chemical that builds up in your brain during the day and makes you feel sleepy. With adenosine blocked, you feel more alert. Caffeine stays in your body for hours: about half of it is still there 5 or more hours later, which is why a late coffee can mess with your sleep. Coffee also contains many other plant compounds, which is one reason scientists study its long-term effects.',
 facts=['A big BMJ review of 200+ studies found people who drank about 3-4 cups a day had a lower risk of dying early and of heart disease than non-drinkers.',
        'That is a link, not proof: coffee drinkers may differ in other ways too.',
        'The US FDA says up to about 400 mg of caffeine a day is generally fine for most healthy adults (roughly 4 cups of brewed coffee).',
        'Pregnant people are usually advised to keep caffeine lower; check with a doctor.',
        'Coffee late in the day can make it harder to fall asleep and reduce deep sleep.',
        'Sugar and syrups can turn a coffee into a dessert, so the toppings matter.'],
 tips_head='SMART COFFEE HABITS',
